what are my favorite top five movies for  entrepreneurs  I'm going to answer that question in  today's episode of luncheon urn so we  also got asked a question from one of my  YouTube viewers love you guys  Moroccan gamer geek who wrote in to say  can you talk about some motivational  movies please or just write them down if  you don't have time so I had a hard time  with this one and was starting to think  which are the movies that I really love  and enjoy because I get a lot out of  movies and almost every move that I  watch I'm always thinking about my  business in my life and try to pull a  piece of it that I might be able to  apply but for this video I'm gonna share  with you the five ones that have had I  think the biggest impact on my business  number one is job starring Ashton  Kutcher and this movie came to me at the  right time I was always a Steve Jobs fan  but I was going through a time at the  moment in my life was trying to figure  out what am I staying for with my  business what are the big things I want  to accomplish I was trying to find my  one word what eventually became my one  were to believe and I saw the movie  first with my wife and then I saw it  with a colleague of mine and a friend  and after watching that movie the first  time I made the decision that I need to  go out and do something bigger it really  inspired me to want to do something more  I wanted people to hit my website and  watch my videos and respond with my  social media posts and feel feel  something that matters I wanted  everybody to come away from every video  that they watch inspired and wanted to  take action because that was the feeling  I got from leaving that movie and I  wanted to be able to instill that in  other people and immediately I made  appointments with two friends of mine  and wanted to do a big project with them  and I didn't wait until you know the  next night or the next day I did it on  the spot leaving the theater while I had  my my moment of boldness and watching a  movie and I said that means with both of  them and one of them came on board the  other one didn't work out one of them  came on board and and has been an  amazing contributor to my business sense  and so it's one of my all-time favorite  movies for entrepreneurs number two for  me was Pursuit of Happyness starring  Will Smith and that movie really taught  me about not giving up he was a guy who  was faced with incredible laws and Chris  Gardner in  he was a single dad and he was homeless  and trying to look after son and try to  just build a positive life and he wanted  to be a stockbroker and you know just  got kicked and punched in the gut over  and over and over and over again and  just stuck to his dream and never gave  up and eventually turned it around  became a millionaire it started living  his life you know built a dream life for  himself and you know no matter what  you're going through right now in your  life it probably is not as bad as what  Chris Gardner had from going from a  homeless single dad and if he could  stick on his dream and he could continue  to plow through every single day just  gives me the courage and the motivation  inspiration say I can do it too  and hopefully you can as well so I  highly recommend watching that movie  number three is Moneyball and that stars  Brad Pitt and I was a fan of the book by  Michael Lewis and what it teaches you is  to be creative and to think outside the  box and do things that are different  because you are forced to be constrained  by your resources it's a story of Billy  Beane who's a general manager of the  Oakland Athletics and it was a team that  had very little money and they're trying  to compete with the New York Yankees and  that the big the big teams in baseball  and they couldn't do it by out spending  and I think it's it's the same thing  with you as an entrepreneur right you've  got your business you're taking on the  New York Yankees in your industry the  big players you're not going to do it by  taking out Super Bowl commercials and by  investing a lot of money in magazine  advertising in TV ads right you can't do  it the same way they do it and so the  lack of resources forces you to be  creative and think outside the zone and  so that's what I really loved about it  was very entrepreneurial to me and one  of my favorite movies  number four is any given Sunday and the  movie itself is just okay but it's  famous to this one epic speech by Al  Pacino  that I remember when I had my company  and I was struggling I would have that  on at least once a day to get me fired  up and excited to go out and do  something important with my business I  had a business partner at the time in  that company and we would recite the  lines to each other and it just was a  fun way to keep ourselves motivated and  amped up and I think the movie itself  again was just okay but it's worth  watching just that one clip of Al Pacino  if you need some motivation to get going  for the day  number five is Seabiscuit and that's  because the movie is all about believe  it's about this horse that was supposed  to do well that never did well and was  just thrown in cast aside it's about  this trainer that used to be a champion  and that nobody wanted to deal with it's  about this jockey that is too big that  nobody thinks going to be a jockey it's  about this owner that is down on his  luck and all of these people who  everybody has discounted and said there  has been xand they're never going to  make it find each other somehow and then  create history by winning a whole bunch  of races and it's based on a true story  and I've seen that movie probably thirty  times it's my favorite movie and I  encourage you to go check it out all  about believe and it's called Seabiscuit  and before wrapping up I wanted to  quickly talk about one movie called  boiler room and when I was a younger  entrepreneur I loved this movie but it's  come off my list I liked how Ben Affleck  came in and would tell the sales  associates about selling and you would  say what did he say he said that a sale  is always made he would say that either  you convince somebody why they need to  buy from you or they convince you on a  reason why they don't need to either way  a sale is made and that got me all  excited and fired up and I thought that  was how you did business through cold  calling and forcing people to buy from  you and convincing them that you had the  right product for them and as I've grown  older I've come to understand that it's  not like that  almost at all and that's one of the  least effective ways to actually grow  your business and so it's fallen off my  top-five list so I hope that helped  thank you guys so much for watching I'd  love to know from you what is your  favorite movie that all entrepreneurs  must watch leave it in the comments  below I'm curious to find out and I'm  gonna join in the discussion thank you  so much for watching  continue to believe and I'll see you  soon
 

